# Upstream-owned files the fork changes anyway, and why.
#
# fork-check.sh fails on any difference from upstream that is not in
# .fork/overlay, .fork/removed or here, so this file is the whole inventory of
# what the fork changes in shared code. It reports an entry as stale once the
# file matches upstream again (typically: the upstream PR merged and the sync
# brought it in); fork-sync.sh drops stale entries in the merge commit.
#
# Every group says when it goes away. "Never" is allowed, with a reason.
# One glob per line; `*` also matches `/`.
#
# A heading starting `## [take-upstream #N ...]` marks a group whose upstream
# PR differs from the fork's copy (review changes, or a deliberate redesign).
# Once upstream contains the squash commit of every listed PR, fork-sync.sh
# resolves a conflict in those files to upstream's side: the resolution a
# person would make when the PR lands. Until then, and for any group without
# the marker, a conflicting carry stops the sync.

